you  [Music]  this morning in our pushing-the-limit  series we introduced you to a wounded  Army veteran who overcame incredible  odds to climb one of the world's most  treacherous mountains now Kilimanjaro is  Africa's highest peak at more than  19,000 feet but Adam Keyes recently made  it to the summit on prosthetic legs he  lost three limbs in Afghanistan and had  never climbed a mountain before he's  documented the trip for CBS this morning  Jan Crawford followed his journey Jan  good morning well good morning John I  mean this is an incredible story of  perseverance and faith and really just  making the most out of every minute that  you have I mean Adam Keyes says his  story shows you can do things that no  one would have ever thought possible if  you believe you can for anyone is a  soaring achievement but for Adam Keyes  the summit of Mount Kilimanjaro was only  part of the climb a few years ago taking  even one step was unimaginable on July  14th 2010 was the day I was injured he  was 26 years old an Army paratrooper on  patrol in Afghanistan when his truck  parked by a roadside bomb I said I open  the door the ID went off so that's the  last I remember he woke up two months  later for a while there it was  touch-and-go for a long long time  doctors initially gave Keyes less than a  1% chance of survival but to his mother  that meant he had a chance she came in  and the doctor said do you know how this  is I said yes that's my mother  she started crying and I you know I'm  getting teared up right now but she just  looked at the doctor and said I told you  so it was one of those things and what  did she know about you that they didn't  I guess mostly I am determined and when  I set my set my goals are something I  want to get it done but mostly I'm  stubborn and I like to prove people  wrong months went by before Keyes was  strong enough to learn the other  soldiers hadn't made it and that hit me  very hard I felt like I left my  because you know your team over there  and you uh some guys looked up to me and  not for much reason because I didn't  have the rank or anything but I was  always determined you're a leader I  tried to be I always wanted to be that's  for sure  enduring over 100 surgeries Keyes was  determined to survive for them i coded  six times I should have been dead by any  stretch of the imagination I should have  been dead but I was given a second  chance smile it was a journey of hope  and determination he learned to talk and  walk again and decided he would squeeze  the most out of that second chance  eventually he was competing on the hand  cycle in marathons before turning his  sights incredibly to the peaks of  kilimanjaro it's crazy  it is it's ambitious climbing mount I  mean most people with all four limbs in  the world could never do that well  that's a little bit of Drive again I  mean I use that as motivation to myself  as if it was easy everybody would do it  he saw that climb over 19,000 feet as a  message to others you're pushing  yourself to the limits to show people oh  yeah they can do it if you had that  obstacle in front of you you can't get  over it and I'm hoping to show that you  can do it but it does take a lot of work  you have to lean on other people it's  been Tyler a medic climbed alongside him  documented the trip for us feeling great  to the top a group of Tanzanian guides  became invested in his journey  at times carrying his mountain eyes  wheelchair and lifting his spirits  during the hardest moments they imagined  it would be brutal the outcome uncertain  but after five days of climbing on sore  limbs followed by freezing nights they  reached the top 2010 airborne all the  way and there at the summit Kees felt  even closer to his fellow soldiers his  goal now help others back home and  landing back in the US news of his  achievement spread quickly in the  airport and Kees got the recognition he  never seeks but deserves  now Adam isn't stopping there he got  back from the climb last week and then  he traveled to Chicago for the marathon  there this past weekend and now he's  getting ready to compete in the New York  City Marathon next month and at the same  time he's also raising money for wounded  warriors through his Facebook page this  is all part of an effort that he's  calling one step forward beyond oh wow I  mean what can't he do he said it was  theory but now we all want to meet him  Jan and a pool crying here on set  because he's just incredible to leave  that Purple Heart there to Wow you want  to meet him yes  what a hero Wow amazing

